{
  "case_id": "2002-01-896",
  "document_hash": "3348d4b4178577c75f809ea166385397a52a33df962795a30b3db3274278eb73",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Supreme Court",
      "case_id": "2002-01-896",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3348d4b4178577c75f809ea166385397a52a33df962795a30b3db3274278eb73",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3348d4b41785-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:04 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2002-01-896",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3348d4b4178577c75f809ea166385397a52a33df962795a30b3db3274278eb73",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3348d4b41785-0000-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "That is, there is an appeal in this case from the dismissal with prejudice of a consolidated complaint which was filed voluntarily by the plaintiffs in this case seeking -- as against plaintiffs that are different than any -- than the constituent State court actions, including different plaintiffs seeking different causes of action and seeking a different form of relief, that is, specific performance of the Ford rebate program."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ford Motor Company and Citibank (Petitioners)",
      "case_id": "2002-01-896",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3348d4b4178577c75f809ea166385397a52a33df962795a30b3db3274278eb73",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3348d4b41785-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So let me first address why I think the Ninth Circuit and this Court had appellate jurisdiction, and it is straightforward."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2002-01-896",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3348d4b4178577c75f809ea166385397a52a33df962795a30b3db3274278eb73",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3348d4b41785-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: -- not -- not the person who benefitted by the dismissal and that --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ford Motor Company and Citibank (Petitioners)",
      "case_id": "2002-01-896",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3348d4b4178577c75f809ea166385397a52a33df962795a30b3db3274278eb73",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3348d4b41785-0000-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Now, it is clear that a prevailing party normally cannot appeal, but the operative word that this Court has recognized, at least since the Electrical Fittings case, is normally. And this Court and the lower courts have applied a rather particular test to determine when normally doesn't apply, and that is, is the nominally prevailing party sufficiently aggrieved by a decision in its favor that it retains a, quote, stake in the appeal? And that's -- that test was articulated by this Court in Guaranty Trust v. Roper -- QUESTION: Mr. Waxman. MR. WAXMAN: -- and in Forney v. Apfel."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2002-01-896",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3348d4b4178577c75f809ea166385397a52a33df962795a30b3db3274278eb73",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3348d4b41785-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Because you have --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Waxman",
      "case_id": "2002-01-896",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3348d4b4178577c75f809ea166385397a52a33df962795a30b3db3274278eb73",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3348d4b41785-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I don't know of any such action. I don't know of any district judge who has done that at the urging of a defendant."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2002-01-896",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3348d4b4178577c75f809ea166385397a52a33df962795a30b3db3274278eb73",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3348d4b41785-0001-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the court, after consideration of class certification, wrestling with -- I think with the laws of the various different States that were invoked by that motion, said to us, I'm issuing an order to show cause why I shouldn't, on my own motion, dismiss this case for lack of jurisdiction."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Berman",
      "case_id": "2002-01-896",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3348d4b4178577c75f809ea166385397a52a33df962795a30b3db3274278eb73",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3348d4b41785-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It is not uncommon in MDL practice for pretrial purposes to try to organize the pleadings for the district judge in a consolidated complaint."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Berman",
      "case_id": "2002-01-896",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3348d4b4178577c75f809ea166385397a52a33df962795a30b3db3274278eb73",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3348d4b41785-0002-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "out to us that this order was actually stayed by the district court and by the Ninth Circuit, and I don't find the stay order in the appendix."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2002-01-896",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3348d4b4178577c75f809ea166385397a52a33df962795a30b3db3274278eb73",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3348d4b41785-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the proposition that's asserted here today by the petitioners that you can use administrative costs in satisfying the amount in controversy would vastly expand the Federal caseload"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Amicus",
      "case_id": "2002-01-896",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3348d4b4178577c75f809ea166385397a52a33df962795a30b3db3274278eb73",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3348d4b41785-0002-0004",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the amicus seem to suggest that plaintiffs never want to be in Federal court."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Seth P. Waxman",
      "case_id": "2002-01-896",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3348d4b4178577c75f809ea166385397a52a33df962795a30b3db3274278eb73",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3348d4b41785-0002-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Thank you, Your Honor. I'll use 1 minute on appellate jurisdiction and I hope 2 minutes on subject matter jurisdiction."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2002-01-896",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3348d4b4178577c75f809ea166385397a52a33df962795a30b3db3274278eb73",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3348d4b41785-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Why -- Mr. -- Mr. Waxman, if you -- if the test is one plaintiff -- if it's Judge Posner's test, you assume there's one plaintiff, what will it cost the defendant."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
